DONE: reading is robust.
  - PIO flux_reader measures full period (pulse + gap)
  - CRC-16/CCITT on ID and data fields
  - Static PLL with half-cell tracking
  - 500ms read timeout, single external retry
  - Core 0: USB MSC + CDC debug + LED (RGB)
  - Core 1: pure floppy I/O, no peripherals
  - Step 3ms/3ms, settling 8ms, motor spin-up 500ms
  - DIP switch mode flags (debug/write/greasweasel)
  - WP pin + DIP switch write-protect layering
  - Composite USB device (MSC + CDC ACM)
  - DEBUG.md reference for serial output

DONE: write corruption fixes (4 bugs)
  1. emit_byte_a1() bit4: clock present [1,0] -> should be absent [0,0]
  2. PIO out_shift direction: shift_right=true output LSB-first but
     encoder packs MSB-first, reversing every 32-bit word.
  3. Second CRC byte prev chaining: used (crc>>7)&1 instead of (crc>>8)&1
  4. PIO clock divider: clkdiv=10 gave 1us half-cells, but decoder
     PLL=200 targets 2us. Changed to clkdiv=20.

DONE: GreaseWeasel raw flux mode (DIP bit 2)
  - Full GW protocol via CDC ACM: 22 commands implemented
  - Flux read (CMD_READ_FLUX): index-synced + ticks_limit + RPM modes
  - Revs-based capture with 160KB RAM buffer, greasepack encoding
  - Hardware GPIO IRQ-based index pulse detection
  - IRQ-based index detection restored in revs-based capture (was polling)
  - Pin-level noise filter on index accept (gpio_get==0 check)
  - gw read: working on all tracks, sectors decoding confirmed
  - RPM mode: correct on all disks (synthetic flux from index pulse timing,
    avoids RDATA-to-INDEX crosstalk by not using real PIO flux data)
  - gw seek / gw info: functional
  - SET_PARAMS / GET_PARAMS: 8x uint16 delay parameters implemented
  - GET_INDEX_TIMES: proper implementation
  - ERASE_FLUX: write-protect check + configurable motor delay
  - FluxEngine: reads and decodes sectors
  - USB descriptors: VID 0x1209 PID 0x4d69, "gw-compat" product string
  - Serial number from RP2040 unique board ID
  - 10s motor auto-off watchdog
  - CDC TX buffer increased to 512 bytes

DONE: CMD_WRITE_FLUX protocol fixes (2024-06)
  - greaseunpack 2-byte decoding formula corrected (was base-250, now
    proper base-255: (data-250)*255+(data2-1)+250)
  - gw_init_writer: only disables SM if currently claimed
  - TX FIFO pre-load: single short pulse (~4.7us) instead of 4x1.3ms
  - dv minimum clamp: dv<2 -> dv=2 (prevents PIO jmp x-- infinite loop)
  - WDATA restored to HIGH after write (was left LOW by stalled PIO)
  - gw_writer SM fully released (unclaimed + program removed) after write
  - Write-to-read recovery delay: 200ms sleep after WGATE drop
  - Read capture loops: added 5s deadline timeouts (prevents lockups)
  - Revs-based capture: restored hardware IRQ index detection (was polling)
  - Revs-based capture: added gpio_get(PIN_INDEX)==0 noise filter to
    prevent RDATA crosstalk glitches from being accepted as index pulses

IN PROGRESS: write verifies correctly but immediate read-after-write fails
  - WRITE PRODUCES CORRECT DATA ON DISK: reset-and-read verifies
    all 18 sectors with correct checksums. The gw_writer PIO and
    greasepack encoding/decoding are working correctly.
  - IMMEDIATE READ-AFTER-WRITE FAILS: The verify step captures only
    ~8-9 sectors (half the track). Read captures ~89ms of data vs
    expected 199ms full revolution.
  - Tried: 5ms/20ms/200ms passive recovery sleep after write (no change)
  - Tried: deselect/reselect drive cycle (shifted alignment, no improvement)
  - Tried: gw_sample_freq at 200MHz (broke reads entirely)
  - Hypothesis: The read amplifier physically saturates during the write
    and needs more recovery time than practical for a tight verify loop.
    May need active write-to-read transition (e.g. dummy read to exercise
    the amp before the real verify).
  - Alternative: Issue may be in the host software's choice of ticks_limit
    for the verify step (FluxEngine uses revs=0 with ticks_limit mode).
    The host may be computing a ticks_limit value that captures only
    half a revolution.

BUG: Any write path corrupts surrounding sectors on formatted disks.
  Both MSC-mode writes (write_physical_sector via flux_writer PIO)
  and GW-mode writes (cmd_write_flux via gw_writer PIO) damage
  adjacent sectors on already-formatted media.
  Suspected causes:
  - WGATE timing: write gate may overlap into next sector's header/gap
    if the bitstream timing doesn't match the physical sector spacing
  - write_physical_sector() does not verify ID CRC before writing;
    only checks C:H:S match. A corrupted ID header could misdirect a write
  - write_physical_sector() and format_physical_track() have no
    write-to-read recovery delay after WGATE drop
  - format_physical_track() (low-level format) has additional bugs:
    bp_flush() does not zero-fill the partial final 32-bit word (garbage
    bits in unused positions become spurious flux transitions).
    No GAP4b (trailing fill) after sector 18 — WGATE drops at end of
    sector 18's GAP3, leaving an unterminated splice.
    emit_byte_a1() in the streaming BitPacker doesn't propagate prev_data
    correctly — each A1 resets the clock-encoding context, potentially
    inserting phantom clock bits on the following byte.

DROPPED (faulty, known hardware issue):
  - Index RPM detection via real PIO flux data on formatted disks:
    shows ~2500 RPM instead of ~350. Suspected RDATA-to-INDEX crosstalk
    on GPIO pins. Works correctly on zero-filled/unformatted media.
    FIXED by using RPM mode (revs=2, ticks_limit=0) which measures
    index pulse interval via time_us_32() polling and generates
    synthetic flux data instead of reading real PIO values.

